//Static Member Functions
A static member function is a member function declared using the static keyword.

class Student {
public:
    static void show() {
        cout << "Hello";
    }
};
How do we call it?

Since it belongs to the class, we can call it without creating an object:

Student::show();
Important rule

A static member function cannot directly access non-static data members.

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    static void show() {
        cout << age;  // ❌
    }
};

Why? Because age belongs to an object, while show() is associated with the class and doesn't have a specific object.

It can access static members:

class Student {
public:
    static int count;

    static void show() {
        cout << count;  // ✅
    }
};
Simple definition

Static member function = a class-level function that can be called using the class name and does not require an object.


